course: dictionary
lesson: 2461
title: Dare: brave, challenge or warning
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The word dare can be a verb (dare, dares, dared, daring) or a noun (a dare). Its main meanings are: to be brave enough to do something, to challenge someone to do something risky, and a dare is that challenge. «Never jump from high walls or roofs for a dare; falls can break bones or worse.» Here a dare is a challenge that can lead to danger.
explain: Dare can also show anger or a strong warning. «How dare you!» means you are cross at someone's rude act. «Don't you dare» means "do not do this, I warn you." Look at the words around dare to find which meaning it carries.
explain: When dare means being brave enough, it is a verb. «Warm parents make mistakes feel fixable, so kids dare to try.» Here kids are brave enough to try. «The Mole hardly dares to look up» means he is so nervous that he almost does not look up. The word hardly means almost not, so it changes the meaning.
fact: Dare is a verb with the forms dares, dared and daring.
fact: A dare, as a noun, is a challenge, such as when a friend says, «I dare you!»
fact: To dare someone means to challenge them to do something risky to show they are brave.
fact: «How dare you!» shows you are angry at someone's rude act.
fact: «Saying «No thanks» and changing the subject is a good way to turn down a dare.» It is brave to say no to a dangerous dare.
exercise: «Never jump from high walls or roofs for a dare; falls can break bones or worse.» In this sentence, what is a dare? | *A challenge to do something risky | A kind of bird that lives on roofs | A soft landing for jumpers | A safe place to play
exercise: «Warm parents make mistakes feel fixable, so kids dare to try.» What does dare mean here? | Shout loudly before trying | *Are brave enough to try | Are angry about trying | Are too tired to try
exercise: Sam's friend says, «I dare you to climb that wall!» What is a good answer? | *«No thanks», and then change the subject | Shout «How dare you!» and then climb it anyway | Climb it at once, because a dare must always be done | Run away and never come back to school
test: What does «Don't you dare» show? | *A strong warning not to do something | A question about how old you are | A sign that you are very tired | A happy invitation to come and play
test: «The Mole hardly dares to look up.» What does this suggest? | He is searching for a dare | He is brave and looks up at once | *He is too nervous to look up properly | He is fast asleep under the ground
exam: Which phrase shows anger at someone's rude act? | I dare you | Daredevil | *How dare you! | Truth or dare
